\section{Correlation Heatmap}

Because our dataset contains only a handful of numerical and boolean features, it was essential to verify whether these variables were redundant or highly correlated. A correlation heatmap was generated to quantify linear dependencies between the engineered features.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.65\textwidth]{Heatmap.png}
    \caption{Correlation heatmap of numerical and boolean features.}
\end{figure}

The heatmap shows very limited correlation between variables, except for a moderate relationship between \texttt{Hour} and \texttt{conn\_per\_hour}. This confirms that each feature captures distinct information and that the small number of columns is the true bottleneck—not multicollinearity. This analysis justifies why simple models already perform very well: the decision boundary is inherently simple.


\section{PCA Visualisation}

To visually inspect how the samples were organised in the feature space, a PCA projection onto two dimensions was performed. The PCA allows us to observe whether Normal (N) and Suspect (S) traffic naturally form separable clusters and where misclassifications occur.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{PCA_2D.png}
    \caption{2D PCA projection, with misclassified points highlighted.}
\end{figure}

The projection reveals that the two classes are mostly separable with only a small overlap region. Misclassified points lie exactly in this overlapping area, indicating that the observed errors are not due to poor model performance but to intrinsic ambiguity in the data itself. This confirms that increasing model complexity is unlikely to provide meaningful improvements.

\section{Decision Tree Interpretation}

A shallow Decision Tree (max depth = 3) was trained to obtain an interpretable model and understand the hierarchical structure of the decision process.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{Decision_Tree_for_Connection_Type.png}
    \caption{Decision tree for \texttt{connection\_type}.}
\end{figure}

The tree reveals that the classification relies primarily on two variables: \texttt{preauth} and \texttt{conn\_per\_hour}. A single split on these features already separates the majority of Suspect connections from Normal ones. This confirms that the problem is linearly (and almost trivially) separable, which explains why Logistic Regression, SVM, Random Forest and Gradient Boosting all achieve similar results. The tree therefore validates our hypothesis that the dataset is simple rather than the models being exceptionally powerful.


\section{Random Forest and Gradient Boosting}

Ensemble tree-based models were also trained for comparison. The Random Forest classifier stabilises predictions by aggregating many decision trees trained on bootstrapped samples, while Gradient Boosting sequentially corrects previous errors.

